Based on my review of the code and documentation, here's how to set up your first rectangles (rects) to define locations:
Setting Up Rectangles
Rectangles define geographic areas (like "home", "work", "gym", etc.) that map lat/lon coordinates to named locations. The system uses these to determine where people are when their physical clock hands point to locations.
Steps:
1. Access the rectangle editor by navigating to:
      https://www.locograph.majen.net/rects.html
      You'll need to authenticate with your credentials (e.g., so:jomp90staffle).
2. Draw rectangles on the map:
   - Click the "Draw Rectangle" button
   - Click and drag on the Google Map to create a rectangle around a location
   - A prompt will appear asking you to name the rectangle (e.g., "home", "work", "gym")
   - The rectangle will be editable (you can resize and move it)
3. Edit existing rectangles:
   - Double-click any rectangle to rename it
   - Enter an empty name to delete a rectangle
   - Drag rectangles to reposition them
   - Drag the corners/edges to resize them
4. Save your rectangles:
   - Click the "Save Rectangles" button when done
   - You should see a success message
How Rectangles Work:
From src/common.ts:63-93, the system:
- Checks if a user's velocity is > 6 km/h (slow jog) → labels them as "moving"
- Otherwise, finds all rectangles containing the user's lat/lon coordinates
- If multiple rectangles overlap, selects the smallest one by area (most specific location)
- If no rectangle matches → labels as "unknown"
- Returns the angle for that label from your CLOCK_CONFIG environment variable
Data Format:
Rectangles are stored as objects with these properties (src/storage/interfaces.ts:10-16):
{
  name: string,    // e.g., "home", "work"
  north: number,   // northern latitude boundary
  south: number,   // southern latitude boundary  
  east: number,    // eastern longitude boundary
  west: number     // western longitude boundary
}
The rectangles are stored in DynamoDB in the OwnTracksLocations table with username='rects' and friend_group='system'.
